-- s 10. HYPOTHETICAL: Sam sues a Canberra retailer in the Federal Court over an unsafe heater (ACL part 3-3); the Magistrates
-- Court could hear all of it.
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `FedC` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `possibly: the court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT court that has jurisdiction over all of it (such as the Magistrates Court), on my application or of its own initiative (s 10)`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `ACT SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `possibly: the court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT court that has jurisdiction over all of it (such as the Magistrates Court), on my application or of its own initiative (s 10)`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `NSW SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `possibly: the court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT court that has jurisdiction over all of it (such as the Magistrates Court), on my application or of its own initiative (s 10)`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `FedC` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: no matter in my proceeding arises under the Australian Consumer Law parts section 10 names (s 10(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `FedC` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: a matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter (s 10(c))`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `FedC` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 10 does not apply to an appeal (s 10(d))`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `FedC` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: no ACT court other than the Supreme Court has jurisdiction over all the matters in my proceeding (s 10(e))`
#ASSERT `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` `ACT lower` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 11. HYPOTHETICAL: Mei's ACT claim, now in the NSW Supreme Court after a transfer.
-- Her claim under the ACT's Civil Law (Wrongs) Act 2002 (a written law of another State, from NSW's seat): s 11(1)(b).
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE TRUE `NSW SC` EQUALS `answered` `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
-- A common law claim: the law of NSW, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE FALSE `NSW SC` EQUALS `answered` `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
-- In the Federal Court: where the matter was first commenced in or transferred to it (s 11(2)).
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE FALSE `FedC` EQUALS `answered` `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
-- s 11(1)(b) comes before s 11(2) even in a federal court.
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` TRUE TRUE `FamC` EQUALS `answered` `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim question` FALSE TRUE `NSW SC` EQUALS `not covered` `the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction, so section 11 does not say which law it applies`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 11(3), 12, 13. After the transfer.
#ASSERT `What carries over after transfer question` FALSE EQUALS `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`
#ASSERT `What carries over after transfer question` TRUE EQUALS `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer or removal have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to` EQUALS `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c)` EQUALS `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c) (s 13(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision question` `some other decision` EQUALS `not covered` `this Act says nothing of that decision`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 14. HYPOTHETICAL: Mei wins in the Federal Court on her ACT matter and wants to enforce in the ACT.
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` `FedC` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my Federal Court or Family Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in that court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` `FamC` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my Federal Court or Family Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in that court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` `ACT SC` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: my ACT Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
-- A NSW Supreme Court judgment: s 14 does not speak to it.
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` `NSW SC` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
-- Not given under cross-vested jurisdiction: s 14 is not needed.
#ASSERT `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` `FedC` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
